/*!
\page vt_page_front Vt : Value Types
\if ( PIXAR_MFB_BUILD )
\mainpage Vt : Value Types
\endif

\section vt_overview Overview

Defines classes that provide for type abstraction (VtValue) and enhanced array types (VtArray).  The Vt library also provides functions for manipulating value types.  This library operates on the level of language data types and there are differences in the C++ and Python interfaces.

\section vt_value Type Erasure with VtValue
The VtValue class wraps type objects (float, int, bool, GfVec3d, and so on) in a type-agnostic container that includes functions for determining the content type within the container.  The VtValue class is found in the C++ API only, since Python does not have the strong type restrictions of C++.  Python to C++ type conversion is handled automatically by the system.  

\section vt_python_registry Python conversion registry

Vt_ValueFromPythonRegistry owns the shared from-Python conversion registry in
the C++ library. Its header, vt/valueFromPython.h, accepts plain callback
pointers and does not include a binding implementation. Include vt/value.h
separately when defining callbacks that return VtValue.

Boost.Python modules should include vt/boost/valueFromPythonBoost.h and link
the CMake interface target vtBoostPython. It supplies the binding requirements
and selects Vt or the configured monolithic library; it adds no compiled
library. The existing VtValueFromPython<T>() and VtValueFromPythonLValue<T>()
helpers now live in that adapter header rather than vt/valueFromPython.h.
Modules register callbacks into the one core registry, including registrations
from downstream modules; they do not link against the _vt module.

Callbacks borrow their input, execute with the GIL held, and return an empty
value on a non-match. A failed conversion sets a Python exception and returns
an empty value; the module caller must propagate that exception. The Boost
adapter translates error_already_set into this contract. Registered callback
code must remain loaded for the registry's lifetime. New registrations
invalidate cached lvalue choices and take effect on the next invocation.

Requested-type conversion uses RegisterTyped/InvokeTyped independently of
generic type inference. A typed callback returns a success flag separately
from its VtValue, so a successful empty value is distinguishable from a
non-match. Failure preserves the caller's output and any Python exception.
VtValueFromPythonTyped<T>() installs the Boost adapter without changing
generic conversion ordering; the existing registration helpers also install
their corresponding typed extraction. Builtins and complete VtValue extraction
are registered by the Vt Python module. This lets native callers retain the
backend's extraction rules instead of substituting VtValue::Cast semantics.

\subsection vt_to_python To-Python dispatch and binding adapters

The value.h, valueRef.h and valueCommon.h headers contain no Boost.Python
includes. Their per-type tables call Vt_ValueToPythonRegistry, declared in
vt/valueToPython.h. Values created in ordinary C++ code before bindings load
use the current registry when converted, including typed and erased proxies.
Vt_GetPythonObjectFromHeldValue accepts a value or a view and returns an owned
TfPyObjWrapper. Empty values produce None; failures leave a Python exception
set, which the caller must check before using the result.

The _vt module installs a Boost runtime lookup and keeps arithmetic, string
and complex conversions in the module. Downstream class registration is found
at conversion time, without negative caching or a required Vt registration.
For types whose conversion is selected by C++ templates rather than Boost's
runtime registry, include vt/boost/valueToPythonBoost.h and call
VtValueToPython<T>(). The from-Python registration helpers also register the
corresponding typed to-Python adapter. Registry callbacks return a new Python
reference or null with an exception set; they run under the GIL and must not
throw binding-specific exceptions into the core.

Include vt/boost/valueBoost.h in binding code that constructs a VtValue from
boost::python::object or accepts VtValueRef arguments. The adapter maps the
Python object to TfPyObjWrapper storage and owns the inline Boost argument
converter that keeps a temporary value alive for the duration of its view.
Include it before the first such construction, even if a core value header
was already included. Missing adapters for Python object managers are rejected
at compile time rather than silently storing a binding object in core values.
The specialization is not installed by including the core headers.

This separation covers the from-Python registry and the value interfaces.
Reusable array binding helpers are installed as vt/boost/wrapArray.h,
vt/boost/wrapArrayEdit.h and vt/boost/pyOperators.h, owned by vtBoostPython.
Use this target for downstream VtWrapArray, VtWrapArrayEdit and sequence-cast
registration. Array names and the legacy shape helper are inline binding code;
modules do not link against _vt to use them.

The buffer producer, buffer consumer and their conversion registrations live
only in _vt. arrayPyBuffer.h is private and is not installed. The unused
VtArrayFromPyBuffer C++ entry point has been removed. Python FromBuffer and
FromNumpy methods keep their existing API and copying behavior. The buffer
implementation uses the full Python API in the module, without changing the
Limited API floor of the core services.

When Python support is enabled, core Vt compiles with Py_LIMITED_API targeting
Python 3.10 or later. That define is private: binding modules use the full API.
The core exports no Boost.Python usage requirements. On Windows it links the
stable Python ABI target rather than a versioned Python runtime.

For a non-monolithic package, find_package(pxr COMPONENTS vt) and the vt target
select the stable core without requesting the bindings' exact Python minor
version on Windows. The vtBoostPython component selects the version-specific
binding requirements. With a monolithic package use usd_m for implementation
or vtBoostPython for bindings; other monolithic components still use the full
Python API. Always use CMake targets rather than assuming output filenames.
Existing non-Python dependency discovery is unchanged. OpenUSD does not bundle
a Python runtime; deployment remains the downstream application's decision.

\section vt_array Shared Arrays - VtArray
The VtArray class represents an arbitrary length homogeneous container.  In the C++ API, the constructor lets you create an array of a specified size.  The VtArray interface on the Python side is implemented as a set of typed array classes (for example, BoolArray, StringArray, Vec4dArray).
*/
